dir
description: A plain filesystem directory — an object with keyed entries
- >
  A **directory** is a mapping: FILENAMES are the keys, and the entries are
  [blobs](::language:concretes:00-storage:02-binary_files) or nested documents — the
  [storage axis](::language:concretes:00-storage) names the concretes.
  A plain dir parses into the abstract as an OBJECT with keyed elements, and nothing
  more: it cannot BE a list — disk has no order — and it cannot carry a scalar self-value; the
  control `.yo` subdirectory lifts both limits (see the case concretes' subchapters). Scalar
  CHILDREN are fine — a small file is a scalar:
- !!<format: text/plain> |
  julia/
    name       — "Julia"
    age        — 42
    pets/
      cat      — "Whiskers"
- >
  Case sensitivity of the keys is the filesystem's, not the language's — the two concretes
  below; the entry NAME is a construct of its own; an entry's value can be ANY yamlover
  representation — the grammar recurses here:
- *: 00-dir_posix
- *: 01-dir_win
- *: 02-filename
- *..
